The issue you're encountering is due to the fact that Spring's RestTemplate is trying to deserialize the response body into a `Doctor` object, but the response content type is `application/xml`, which doesn't match the expected content type for `Doctor`. This results in a `RestClientException` because the `RestTemplate` can't find a suitable `HttpMessageConverter` to handle the `Doctor` class.

To fix this, you need to provide a custom `HttpMessageConverter` that can handle the `Doctor` class. This custom converter should map the `Doctor` class to the desired XML representation.

Here's a step-by-step guide to solving this issue:

### Step 1: Create a Custom `HttpMessageConverter`

```java
public class DoctorMessageConverter implements MessageConverter {

    @Override
    public boolean supports(Class<?> clazz) {
        return Doctor.class.equals(clazz);
    }

    @Override
    public String readEntityToString(Reader reader) throws IOException {
        try {
            ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
            return mapper.writeValueAsString(mapper.readValue(reader, Doctor.class));
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Could not read entity as string", e);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void writeEntities(List entities) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Not implemented");
    }

    @Override
    public <T> T readFrom(Class<T> clazz, MediaType type, MultivaluedMap<String, String> headers, InputStream content) throws IOException {
        try {
            ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
            return mapper.readValue(content, clazz);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Could not read entity from InputStream", e);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public <T> T readFrom(Class<T> clazz, MediaType type, MultivaluedMap<String, String> headers, String text) throws IOException {
        try {
            ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
            return mapper.readValue(text, clazz);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Could not read entity from text", e);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public boolean supports(Class<?> clazz, MediaType type) {
        return supports(clazz);
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Register the Custom `HttpMessageConverter`

Add the `DoctorMessageConverter` to the Spring `RestTemplate`:

```java
@Component
public class RestTemplateConfig {

    @Autowired
    private ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    @Bean
    public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
        HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory requestFactory = new HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory();
        requestFactory.setConnectTimeout(10000); // 10 seconds
        requestFactory.setReadTimeout(10000);

        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.configure(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS, false);
        mapper.configure(DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES, false);
        mapper.configure(DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_NULL_BEANS, false);

        RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate(requestFactory);
        restTemplate.getMessageConverters().add(new DoctorMessageConverter());

        return restTemplate;
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Use the Custom `HttpMessageConverter` in Your Code

Replace the `HttpEntity` initialization with the custom `DoctorMessageConverter`:

```java
